\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}
\title{Energy Functions for Protein Folding}
\author{S. Adeyemi and R. Castillo}
\date{April 2026}

Contact prediction has improved rapidly since the introduction of coevolutionary features.
Modern predictors combine multiple sequence alignments with attention based architectures,
and they now reach useful accuracy even for shallow alignment depths.

Folding a sequence into a three dimensional structure remains a search problem.
Energy functions guide the search toward conformations that are physically plausible,
while geometric constraints keep bond lengths and angles within realistic ranges.

An open question is how much of the final accuracy comes from the energy model itself
and how much comes from the specific search procedure used at inference time.

